<div class="article-body">

<blockquote>
<p>I interviewed 12 families of only-child parents born in the 1990s. One thing kept coming up: they weren't teaching their kids — they were making up for what they themselves didn't have growing up.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Yuanyuan's mom (born 1990, Shanghai) bought her first AI learning tablet when her daughter was three and a half. She's an only child too.</p>

<p>"My mom bought me the complete set of <em>Children's Pictorial</em> and subscribed for three years. But no one taught me how to read it. In the end it all went to waste."</p>

<p>She bought the most expensive brand on the market — nearly 4,000 yuan. "I thought, nobody taught me when I was little. Now there's a machine that can teach my kid. I have to get the best one." Two months later, her daughter was using it to draw and watch cartoons.</p>

<p>This isn't an isolated story.</p>

<p>In Q1 2026, China's learning tablet market sold 1.252 million units. Despite a 1.0% year-on-year decline, spending by middle-class families on AI education hardware kept climbing. Homework Hero (Zuoyebang) captured 32.9% market share (up 4.6pp), with sales in the 2,000-3,000 yuan price range exceeding the combined sales of positions 2 through 4 (source: RotoTech, 2026-05-13). Xueersi followed at 20.1%, iFLYTEK at 18.5%.</p>

<p>But the real question isn't "should I buy one?" It's "what happens after I do?"</p>

<hr />

<h2>The Data Behind the Anxiety</h2>

<p>76.3% of parents rank AI teaching depth as their top criterion when choosing a learning tablet (source: RotoTech, 2026-05-13). That number says one thing: parents know exactly what they're paying for — not hardware, but the machine's ability to teach.</p>

<p>NetEase Youdao's Q1 2026 AI subscription revenue surpassed 100 million yuan, up 70% year-on-year (source: Youdao Q1 2026 earnings report, 2026-05-21). Subscription models are becoming mainstream — parents are no longer buying a "device," they're buying a "service."</p>

<p>Meanwhile, problems are surfacing. In May 2026, Anhui Province Consumer Protection Commission released an evaluation showing that AI learning tablets make grading errors and produce formulaic essay comments (source: Anhui Consumer Protection Commission, 2026-05-27).</p>

<p>And a Ministry of Education survey (2026-06-01) revealed that 63.8% of children first encounter the internet before age 10, with 6.1% first going online before age 3.</p>

<p>The picture is clear: parents want the best — buy the most expensive device, subscribe to the most comprehensive courses. The result: devices gather dust, AI grading is unreliable, and children get hooked on screens too early.</p>

<p>The problem isn't "to buy or not to buy." It's "how to use."</p>

<hr />

<h2>AI Is a Ladder, Not an Elevator</h2>

<p>Many parents treat AI education products like an elevator — put the child in, press a floor, and done. This is the biggest misconception.</p>

<p>AI is a ladder. It helps you reach things you couldn't otherwise reach. But you still have to climb.</p>

<p>What you need isn't "a machine that can teach my kid for me." It's a tool that makes the learning process visible, trackable, and adjustable.</p>

<blockquote>
<p><strong>Quick check:</strong> Ask one question to judge any AI product: Does it teach the child <em>how to think</em>, or does it just tell the child <em>what the answer is</em>? The former guides process. The latter reinforces outcomes.</p>
</blockquote>

<hr />

<h2>The Three Screening Questions</h2>

<p>If you're torn about whether to buy an AI education product, ask yourself three questions:</p>

<h3>1. Does it teach knowledge or method?</h3>
<ul>
<li>Knowledge ✗: "3+5=8" (child just memorizes)</li>
<li>Method ✓: "How could you break down 3+5? Make a ten first, or split the smaller number?" (child needs to think through the process)</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Does it give feedback or just a score?</h3>
<ul>
<li>Score-only ✗: "3 wrong, 70% correct"</li>
<li>Feedback ✓: "Question 2 had the right idea, but you skipped a calculation step"</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Can you see the process?</h3>
<ul>
<li>Black box ✗: Child answers → machine gives score → next question</li>
<li>Transparent ✓: Logs every step → shows the thinking path → you can replay</li>
</ul>

<blockquote>
<p><strong>Action tip:</strong> Before buying, spend 5 minutes running the three screening questions. If all three answers are ✗, it's probably a problem-drilling machine, not a learning tool.</p>
</blockquote>

<hr />

<h2>The Three-Layer Companion Method</h2>

<p>Here's a practical framework for putting AI products in the right place in family education:</p>

<h3>Layer 1: Parent-Led (Core) | 15 minutes/day</h3>
<p>In this layer, the human matters more than the machine.</p>
<ul>
<li>No AI: parent reads a story or works through a problem with the child</li>
<li>Goal: build the understanding that "learning = someone is here with me"</li>
<li>This is what AI cannot replace</li>
</ul>

<h3>Layer 2: Human-AI Collaboration | 20 minutes/day</h3>
<p>In this layer, human + AI works better than either alone.</p>
<ul>
<li>Child practices with AI while parent observes the process</li>
<li>Key observation: where does the child get stuck? How long does attention last?</li>
<li>Turn AI's report into conversation: "How did you think through this problem?"</li>
</ul>

<h3>Layer 3: AI-Assisted (Free) | 3x/week, 10-15 minutes each</h3>
<p>In this layer, the machine accompanies while the parent manages the boundaries.</p>
<ul>
<li>Child independently uses AI for review or preview</li>
<li>Parent doesn't participate but knows what's being learned (weekly report, time distribution)</li>
<li>Core mechanism: child does a "one-sentence summary" after each session — parent records it</li>
</ul>

<blockquote>
<p><strong>Action tip:</strong> Stick a three-column chart on the fridge. Each evening, spend 3 minutes marking which layer you used today. After one week, you'll see your family's real AI usage pattern.</p>
</blockquote>

<hr />

<h2>Replace Score-Tracking with Question-Tracking</h2>

<p>The most common mistake parents make: treating AI products as a score tracker. "How many points today?" "Did you improve this week?"</p>

<p>This mindset makes "score" the endpoint of learning. A better alternative: pay attention to your child's <em>questioning ability</em>.</p>

<p><strong>Weekly questioning checklist:</strong></p>
<table>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>Beginner (1pt)</th>
<th>Intermediate (2pt)</th>
<th>Advanced (3pt)</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Depth of questioning</td>
<td>Asks one "why"</td>
<td>Follows up 2-3 rounds</td>
<td>Turns it into a mini-investigation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Question variety</td>
<td>Only asks "what is"</td>
<td>Also asks "why/how"</td>
<td>Also asks "what if not"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Expression completeness</td>
<td>Keyword only</td>
<td>Complete sentence</td>
<td>Describes full context</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Resource use</td>
<td>Only reads AI's answer</td>
<td>Asks AI for "more"</td>
<td>Cross-verifies with other tools</td>
</tr>
</table>

<blockquote>
<p><strong>Action tip:</strong> Friday evening, pull out a sheet of paper and look at the AI tool report together. Don't check scores — check whether the questioning level went up. Reward <em>questioning</em>, not scoring.</p>
</blockquote>

<hr />

<h2>Learn AI Yourself First</h2>

<p>This may be the most counterintuitive advice, but it's the most effective: learn to use AI yourself before teaching your child to use it.</p>

<p>Children imitate how you interact with AI. If you only type "what's the answer," your child will too. If you follow up, question, combine information — so will your child.</p>

<p>A three-week starter plan for parents:</p>
<ul>
<li>Week 1: Use AI for one work task — write an email draft, summarize meeting notes, look up a term. Then verify whether it's correct.</li>
<li>Week 2: Ask AI an open-ended question (like "how would you explain photosynthesis to a 5-year-old"). Then follow up three rounds of "anything else?"</li>
<li>Week 3: Let your child watch your complete AI workflow — ask → wait → verify → revise → finalize. Tell them: "AI makes mistakes too, so we check."</li>
</ul>

<blockquote>
<p><strong>Action tip:</strong> Spend 5 minutes a day using a free AI tool for one small daily task. After one week, you'll have enough confidence to say "I know where it's good and where it fails."</p>
</blockquote>

<hr />

<h2>Create a Weekly AI Review Ritual</h2>

<p>Friday evening. 15 minutes. No scoring. Just conversation.</p>

<p><strong>The 15-minute review template:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>First 5 min: "What did you use AI for this week?" (let the child talk, don't interrupt)</li>
<li>Next 5 min: "What did you do well? What felt strange?" (guide expression)</li>
<li>Last 5 min: "What do you want to try with AI next week?" (set a small goal together)</li>
</ul>

<p>If your child says "AI skipped a step on this problem" — that's the best feedback. They're observing AI critically, which goes beyond "just finish it."</p>

<hr />

<h2>A Note to Only-Child Parents</h2>

<p>You're anxious because you only get one shot. In multi-child families, there's a psychological "risk diversification" — if one child doesn't work out, there's another. Only-child families don't have that. Every expectation, every anxiety, every resource piles onto one person.</p>

<p>And that's exactly why you need to stay calm. Your child doesn't need an anxious companion. They need a calm observer.</p>

<p>You don't need to try every AI education product. You just need to find the one that fits your family's rhythm. You don't need to pile on "coding thinking" + "English immersion" + "logic training" before your child turns three. You just need to read them a book for 10 minutes every day.</p>

<blockquote>
<p><strong>Action tip for when anxiety takes over:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Unfollow the product reviewers (they profit from your anxiety)</li>
<li>Close your social feeds (you're not comparing education — you're comparing consumption)</li>
<li>Open a blank document and write: "What do I want my child to be like in ten years?" The answer is probably not "accepted to Tsinghua" — it's "curious, and knows how to find answers." If that's the answer, most AI product evaluations need a reset.</li>
</ol>
</blockquote>

